  "payload": "Comet flyby spacecraft with dust shield and 10 instruments (960 kg at launch)",
  "status": "Success",
  "significance": "Giotto was Europe's first deep-space mission and flew closer to Halley's Comet than any other spacecraft, passing just 596 km from the nucleus. Its images showed for the first time what a comet nucleus looks like: a dark, peanut-shaped body about 15 km long, blacker than coal, with only a few active patches venting jets of gas and dust. It survived a sandblasting by dust that knocked it off balance, was steered back to Earth for a gravity assist, and in 1992 became the first spacecraft to visit two comets, flying within about 200 km of Grigg–Skjellerup.",
  "statusNote": "ESA mission, launched 1985-07-02 on Ariane 1 flight V14. Passed Halley's nucleus at 596 km on 1986-03-14 ~00:03 UT; a dust impact seconds before closest approach tilted its spin axis and the camera was lost. Retargeted to Earth, flew by at 22,730 km on 1990-07-02, then passed comet 26P/Grigg–Skjellerup at ~200 km on 1992-07-10 at 15:30:43 UT (Giotto Extended Mission report, COSPAR 1994, in NASA PDS) (relative speed ~14 km/s), and was switched off on 1992-07-23. A distant 1999 Earth pass is not modeled. No JPL Horizons ephemeris exists. The two post-Halley legs are multi-revolution (about 5 orbits to the 1990 Earth flyby, 1 more to the comet), which the app's single-revolution Lambert solver can't draw directly, so they were solved offline as multi-revolution two-body transfers between the real endpoints (JPL Horizons positions of Halley, Earth and 26P) and split into sub-orbit arcs at fixedPos waypoints. The revolution counts were chosen so that Giotto's speed relative to Earth is conserved across the 1990 flyby (3.53 vs 3.46 km/s); the result reproduces the real post-Halley orbit (0.73–1.04 AU, 305-day period), a flyby periapsis within 4% of the real altitude, and the ~14 km/s encounter speed at 26P. Small trajectory-correction burns are not modeled. Sources: NASA NSSDCA (COSPAR 1985-056A; nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/planets/cometpage.html), the mission's Wikipedia article and its cited references (Siddiqi 2018, Beyond Earth, NASA SP-2018-4041), and agency pages. Halley/26P encounter details: NSSDCA Giotto page (nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/giotto.html).",
